  <h1>RF Engineering in HFT</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Why this matters for <strong>HFT_practice</strong>: Lesson 0011's latency ladder
    placed cross-market microwave/RF links on Tier 5–6. This lesson opens the box.
    The physics is simple — light travels faster through air than glass — but the
    engineering is full of constraints (line-of-sight, weather, FCC licensing,
    bandwidth) that decide what kinds of strategies can use it and which cannot.
  </p>

  <p class="illus">
    Note: per the new repo guidance, code blocks here are <em>illustrative</em> only —
    the repo doesn't yet have cross-venue RF infrastructure. They can be retrofitted
    once we wire one up.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §1 PHYSICS ===================== -->
  <h2>1. The physics — why RF beats fiber over distance</h2>

  <p>
    All electromagnetic signals travel at the speed of light <code>c</code> ≈
    299,792,458 m/s in vacuum. The catch is what happens when they enter a
    medium: glass slows them down dramatically, air barely at all.
  </p>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Medium</th><th>Refractive index <code>n</code></th><th>Effective speed</th><th>% of <code>c</code></th></tr>
    <tr><td>Vacuum</td><td>1.0000</td><td>299,792 km/s</td><td>100%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Air (dry, sea level)</td><td>1.0003</td><td>~299,700 km/s</td><td>~99.97%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Hollow-core fiber</td><td>~1.005</td><td>~298,300 km/s</td><td>~99.5%</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Conventional silica fiber</td><td>~1.467</td><td>~204,400 km/s</td><td>~68%</td></tr>
  </table>

  <p>
    That's the entire story. <strong>Light through glass moves at about two-thirds
    the speed of light through air.</strong> Over a thousand kilometers between
    exchanges, those few percent compound into several milliseconds — and several
    milliseconds is the entire game in cross-market arbitrage.
  </p>

<pre><span class="com"># Illustrative: minimum one-way latency for a 1180 km link</span>
<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">latency_ms</span>(distance_km: <span class="ty">float</span>, fraction_of_c: <span class="ty">float</span>) -> <span class="ty">float</span>:
    c_kms = <span class="num">299_792.458</span>
    <span class="kw">return</span> distance_km / (c_kms * fraction_of_c) * <span class="num">1000.0</span>

<span class="fn">latency_ms</span>(<span class="num">1180</span>, <span class="num">1.000</span>)   <span class="com"># = 3.94 ms  (vacuum / great-circle floor)</span>
<span class="fn">latency_ms</span>(<span class="num">1180</span>, <span class="num">0.997</span>)   <span class="com"># = 3.95 ms  (microwave through air, line-of-sight)</span>
<span class="fn">latency_ms</span>(<span class="num">1180</span>, <span class="num">0.995</span>)   <span class="com"># = 3.96 ms  (hollow-core fiber, also air-filled)</span>
<span class="fn">latency_ms</span>(<span class="num">1180</span>, <span class="num">0.680</span>)   <span class="com"># = 5.81 ms  (conventional fiber)</span>
</pre>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Real-world overhead</strong> — those are physics minimums. Real fiber
    routes aren't straight (they follow railroads, highways); real microwave routes
    aren't straight either but they're close. Real numbers for Chicago↔Carteret
    one-way: fiber ~6.5–7 ms, microwave ~4.0–4.5 ms. The ~2.5 ms one-way edge is
    what funded an entire industry of tower-builders.
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §2 ROUTES ===================== -->
  <h2>2. The famous routes, with real numbers</h2>

  <p>
    Three corridors dominate the global cross-market HFT business. Each connects
    venues whose products trade against each other.
  </p>

  <div class="path">
