  <p>
    The CME↔NJ corridor is the most famous because it carries the relationship
    between <em>futures</em> (e.g. ES, NQ traded in Aurora) and the <em>cash
    equities</em> they implicitly track (S&amp;P 500, Nasdaq 100 traded in Carteret).
    A move in the futures contract gives an informational edge on the cash basket
    — but only to whoever sees it first.
  </p>

  <div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Industry operators (2024–2025):</strong> McKay Brothers and Anova
    Financial Networks are the dominant common-carrier microwave operators on
    the major US/EU corridors. BSO, Quincy Data, and Jump Trading (in-house)
    also operate routes. McKay's published target for Aurora↔Carteret is
    <strong>&lt;8.00 ms round-trip</strong> end-to-end.
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §3 ENGINEERING ===================== -->
  <h2>3. The engineering — what actually has to be built</h2>

  <h3>Tower spacing and hops</h3>
  <p>
    Microwave links are <strong>line-of-sight</strong> — the curvature of the Earth
    eventually puts the receiver behind the horizon. Practical hop length is
    40–70 km depending on tower height and terrain. So Chicago↔NJ at 1180 km
    needs roughly <strong>20–30 hops</strong> in series, each adding tower-side
    repeater latency (~10s of ns per hop).
  </p>

  <h3>Frequency bands</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Band</th><th>Frequency</th><th>Typical hop</th><th>Bandwidth</th><th>Weather sensitivity</th></tr>
    <tr><td>6 GHz</td><td>5.9–7.1 GHz</td><td>60–80 km</td><td>~30 Mbps</td><td>Very low (rain fade negligible)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>11 GHz</td><td>10.7–11.7 GHz</td><td>40–60 km</td><td>~100 Mbps</td><td>Low</td></tr>
    <tr><td>18 GHz</td><td>17.7–19.7 GHz</td><td>20–40 km</td><td>~300 Mbps</td><td>Moderate (rain fade)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>80 GHz (E-band)</td><td>71–86 GHz</td><td>5–10 km</td><td>1–10 Gbps</td><td>Severe (atmospheric absorption)</td></tr>
  </table>

  <p>
    For HFT, the lower bands (6, 11 GHz) dominate the long-haul backbone because
    they survive rain. The higher bands (E-band) are used for last-mile in/out of
    data centers where higher bandwidth is needed and hops are short. The receiver
    is typically <em>on the roof of the exchange data center</em>, or on a pole
    next to it — extra meters of fiber inside the building waste nanoseconds.
  </p>

  <h3>Fresnel zone</h3>
  <p>
    Between two microwave antennas there's an ellipsoidal volume — the
    <strong>Fresnel zone</strong> — that must be roughly 60% clear of obstructions
    for the link to work. The Fresnel zone is fattest at mid-span. Buildings,
    trees, and even crops growing in summer can intrude. Tower heights and route
    planning are designed around this constraint, and an ice storm bending a
    tower can take a link down for hours.
  </p>

  <h3>Modulation and the latency↔bandwidth tradeoff</h3>
  <p>
    Higher-order modulations (256-QAM, 1024-QAM) carry more bits per symbol but
    need higher signal-to-noise ratio and add encoding/decoding latency. HFT
    operators use lower-order modulations (QPSK, 16-QAM) to minimize latency at
    each hop, at the cost of usable bandwidth.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §4 BANDWIDTH TRADEOFF ===================== -->
  <h2>4. The fundamental bandwidth tradeoff — and what actually gets sent</h2>

  <p>
    A full futures-market data feed is <strong>gigabits per second</strong>. A
    long-haul microwave channel delivers <strong>~100 Mbps</strong>. The math
    doesn't work — you cannot ship full market data over microwave.
  </p>

  <p>
    What HFT firms actually send: <strong>highly compressed signals</strong>
    — sometimes just a few critical price ticks, sometimes a single bit
    ("ES went up or down by more than threshold X"). The remote venue's
    strategy is pre-loaded with everything else and waits for that one bit.
  </p>

  <p>Illustrative wire-format for a microwave-carried signal:</p>

<pre><span class="com">// 8 bytes total: a tick worth racing across the country</span>
<span class="kw">struct</span> <span class="ty">__attribute__</span>((packed)) RfSignal {
    <span class="ty">uint16_t</span> instrument_id;   <span class="com">// 2 bytes — e.g. ES, NQ, CL</span>
    <span class="ty">int16_t</span>  delta_ticks;     <span class="com">// 2 bytes — price change since last frame</span>
    <span class="ty">uint32_t</span> ts_remote_ns;    <span class="com">// 4 bytes — sender's hardware timestamp</span>
};
</pre>

  <p>
    Eight bytes. At 100 Mbps that's a transmission time of ~0.6 microseconds for
    one message, leaving headroom for ~12 million messages per second on the
    channel — but in practice you send a few thousand per second because the
    receiver's strategy only needs the headline moves.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Mental model:</strong> microwave isn't a data pipe; it's a
    <em>messaging channel for compressed alpha</em>. Send the one bit of
    information that wouldn't fit in your already-known model of the world,
    and let the receiving end fill in the rest.
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §5 ECONOMICS ===================== -->
  <h2>5. Economics — the common-carrier model and the tier system</h2>

  <p>
    HFT firms rarely build their own networks. The capex (~$10M+ per route),
    multi-year tower lease negotiations, and FCC/Ofcom licensing make it a
    specialty business. Instead, <strong>common-carrier operators</strong> like
    McKay Brothers and Anova run shared infrastructure and sell access in tiers:
  </p>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Premium tier</h4>
      <p>Lowest latency, guaranteed slot, redundant paths. Used by the largest HFT desks for their primary strategies. Annual cost: undisclosed, but in the seven figures per route.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Standard tier</h4>
      <p>Same wire, but pays slightly higher serialization delay (microseconds, not nanoseconds — but enough to matter). Smaller desks; secondary strategies.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Backup / fiber blend</h4>
      <p>Hybrid path that falls back to hollow-core or conventional fiber when the microwave link is degraded (storm, alignment loss). Bandwidth is higher; latency reverts to fiber tier.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Why common-carrier works</h4>
      <p>The towers, licenses, and amplifiers cost the same whether one firm uses them or twenty do. Charging twenty firms tiered access turns a fixed-cost network into a high-margin business.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>The hollow-core fiber competitor</h3>
  <p>
    Conventional silica fiber slows light to ~0.68c. <strong>Hollow-core fiber</strong>
    runs light through an air-filled channel surrounded by photonic-crystal walls
    — speed is ~99% c. Bandwidth is the same as conventional fiber (~hundreds of
    Gbps), latency is only slightly worse than microwave, and it doesn't care
    about rain or line-of-sight.
  </p>

  <p>
    The current state: hollow-core has been deployed by McKay, DRW, Jump and
    others for CME↔NJ — about 1 ms slower than microwave one-way but with
    1000× more bandwidth. Many HFT firms now run <strong>both</strong>: microwave
    for the bit-of-alpha signal, hollow-core for the bulk market data and the
    rest of the strategy state.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §6 IN THIS PROJECT ===================== -->
  <h2>6. In this project</h2>

  <p>
    This repo has no cross-venue strategy today — the GBM trades a daily-rebalance
    long-only equity portfolio from a single market-data source (yfinance), so
    physics of inter-venue latency is irrelevant. But the natural extension is
    worth flagging.
  </p>

  <p>
    A <em>cross-market</em> arbitrage strategy — say, trading the basis between
    E-mini S&amp;P futures (CME, in Aurora) and the SPY ETF (in Carteret) — would
    necessarily inhabit Tier 5/6 of the latency ladder. The strategy would need:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Colocation in <em>both</em> Aurora and Carteret.</li>
    <li>A microwave or hollow-core path between them (lease, not build).</li>
    <li>An FPGA endpoint on each side speaking a compact signal protocol like the 8-byte struct above.</li>
    <li>A pre-loaded model on the receiving side that knows what to do when "ES moved by X ticks" arrives.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    The decision to take that step is <strong>economic before it is technical</strong>.
    A few million dollars per year of operating cost has to be paid back by the
    spread captured on the arb. That's a question for the strategy team, not the
    engineering team.
  </p>

  <div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Repo honesty:</strong> none of this is in the code today, nor should
    it be soon. The point of this lesson is to give you the right mental model
    for when you read about "microwave HFT" — and for the day this repo eventually
    sprouts a cross-venue branch.
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §7 CONTRASTS ===================== -->
  <h2>7. Often confused with</h2>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Microwave vs Hollow-core fiber</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Microwave</strong>: ~99.97% c through air, narrow bandwidth
        (~100 Mbps), weather-sensitive, line-of-sight, cheap per Mbps.
        <strong>Hollow-core</strong>: ~99% c through air-filled fiber, wide
        bandwidth (~Gbps), weather-immune, follows fiber routes (not necessarily
        straight). Most firms now use both.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Microwave vs Millimeter-wave (E-band)</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Microwave</strong> (3–30 GHz): long hops, narrow bandwidth, weather-tolerant.
        <strong>Millimeter-wave / E-band</strong> (60–80+ GHz): short hops (5–10 km),
        wide bandwidth (gigabits), severe rain fade. HFT uses E-band for last-mile,
        microwave for long-haul.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Latency vs Bandwidth (RF specifically)</h4>
      <p>
        Microwave gives you the lowest latency available <em>but</em> the smallest
        usable bandwidth. Fiber gives you the highest bandwidth <em>but</em>
        slower light. The HFT solution: send tiny signals over microwave, send
        bulk data over hollow-core fiber, run both in parallel.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Common-carrier vs Private link</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Common-carrier</strong> (McKay, Anova): shared towers, sold in
        tiers, multiple HFT firms on the same wire. <strong>Private link</strong>:
        a single firm owns the towers and the licenses. The largest desks
        (Jump, Citadel) sometimes own private routes; most rent from a carrier.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Line-of-sight vs Tropospheric scatter</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Line-of-sight</strong>: receiver must see the transmitter
        directly; Earth's curvature limits hops. HFT exclusively uses this.
        <strong>Tropo scatter</strong>: bounce signal off the upper atmosphere
        for longer hops; much higher latency, never used in HFT.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Refractive index vs Group velocity</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Refractive index</strong> tells you the <em>phase</em> velocity
        of light in a medium. <strong>Group velocity</strong> is what carries
        information and is what matters for latency. In standard fiber they
        track closely; in exotic media (hollow-core, slow-light fibers) they
        can diverge.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

