  <div class="question" data-question>
    <p class="q">Q1. The microwave Chicago↔NJ link beats conventional fiber by ~2.5 ms one-way for a single reason at the physics level. What is it?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Bandwidth is actually LOWER on microwave — typically ~100 Mbps vs ~hundreds of Gbps on fiber. The advantage is latency, not bandwidth.">Microwave links carry more bandwidth than fiber, so packets transmit faster.</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Right. Conventional silica fiber has refractive index ~1.47, so light travels at ~0.68c — about 204,400 km/s. Air has index ~1.0003, so microwaves travel at ~99.97% c. Over 1180 km, that's roughly 2 ms saved by the medium alone, plus another ~0.5 ms because line-of-sight routes are straighter than fiber routes.">Conventional fiber has refractive index ~1.47 (~0.68c), while microwave travels through air at ~0.9997c — the medium itself is the bottleneck.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="There's no Earth-curvature shortcut at this scale. The microwave route is line-of-sight which is straighter than fiber but not curvature-corrected.">Microwave signals follow the curvature of the Earth shorter than fiber.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Exchanges process orders the same way regardless of how they arrived. The latency advantage is in transit between Chicago and New Jersey, not at either endpoint.">Exchanges in Carteret prioritize processing for microwave-delivered packets.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question>
    <p class="q">Q2. Why is the data sent over a long-haul microwave link typically a few bytes per message rather than full market data?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="It's not an FCC rule. The constraint is throughput vs latency in radio engineering — higher modulation rates trade more bits per symbol for slower decode and worse SNR margins.">Because the FCC regulates payload sizes on commercial microwave links.</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Right. A long-haul HFT microwave channel delivers around 100 Mbps usable. Full futures market data is gigabits per second. You can't fit it. The solution is to send a compressed signal (sometimes a single bit: 'ES moved by X') and pre-load the receiving strategy with everything else it needs.">Long-haul microwave delivers ~100 Mbps; full market data is gigabits. You can only ship signals, not full feeds.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Receiver-side decoding adds nanoseconds at most. The dominant constraint is link bandwidth, not decoder speed.">Because the receiver's decoder can't process more than 8 bytes per message in real time.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Encryption isn't the binding constraint; the bandwidth physics of the channel is. Some HFT links don't even encrypt because the message contents are public reference prices.">Because the encryption overhead per message limits the payload size.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question>
    <p class="q">Q3. Hollow-core fiber is ~99% c — only slightly slower than microwave (99.97% c). Why do HFT firms still pay for microwave at all?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Hollow-core fiber is widely deployed by McKay, DRW, Jump, and others on CME↔NJ specifically. Availability is not the issue.">Hollow-core fiber hasn't actually been deployed yet on the major HFT corridors.</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Right. The Chicago↔NJ one-way difference between microwave (~4.0 ms) and hollow-core (~5.0 ms) is roughly 1 ms — a small fraction of fiber's 6.5 ms but a huge edge at HFT scales. At market-data rates of 100k+ messages/sec, 1 ms is thousands of ticks. Firms run BOTH paths: microwave for the alpha signal, hollow-core for the wider bandwidth.">Even ~1 ms one-way difference between microwave and hollow-core matters at HFT scales; firms typically run both paths in parallel.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Both technologies are sold in tiered pricing models. Cost isn't structurally simpler for microwave; if anything microwave has higher operating cost per Mbps.">Microwave is structurally cheaper per route than hollow-core fiber.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Hollow-core fiber is widely used for trading data; bulk market data over hollow-core is now standard practice on CME↔NJ.">Hollow-core fiber is reserved for non-trading data only.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question>
    <p class="q">Q4. Why are long-haul HFT microwave links built in the 6 and 11 GHz bands rather than 80 GHz, even though 80 GHz carries more bandwidth?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="80 GHz E-band IS commercially licensed and widely available for data-center interconnect. The constraint is range and weather, not licensing.">Because 80 GHz isn't commercially licensed in the US.</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Right. At 80 GHz, atmospheric absorption (especially the oxygen-absorption band near 60 GHz, but real attenuation at 80 GHz too) and rain fade limit practical hops to 5–10 km. For a 1180 km route that would need ~200 hops vs ~25 hops at 11 GHz. Each hop adds latency and a failure point. Lower bands give you longer reliable hops; 80 GHz is reserved for last-mile in/out of the colo.">At 80 GHz, atmospheric absorption and rain fade limit practical hops to 5–10 km, requiring hundreds of hops on a long route. Lower-frequency bands allow 40–70 km hops and survive weather.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Antenna size and gain are concerns at higher frequencies but the binding constraint for long-haul is atmospheric absorption and weather, not antenna engineering.">Because 80 GHz requires antennas larger than can be mounted on towers.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Radar interference is a non-issue at 80 GHz versus 6 GHz; if anything the lower bands are more crowded by radar and incumbents.">Because 6 GHz signals can't be jammed by weather radar.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question>
    <p class="q">Q5. You want to extend this repo from a single-venue daily GBM into a cross-venue (CME futures ↔ NYSE equities) arbitrage strategy. Which infrastructure decision dominates?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="A faster GBM model wouldn't matter; the strategy lives or dies on the cross-venue latency edge. The model is the secondary concern.">Train a much faster gradient-boosting model for next-tick prediction.</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Right. Cross-market arbitrage is a Tier 5/6 latency strategy by definition. You must colocate at BOTH venues, lease a low-latency path between them (microwave for the signal, hollow-core for bulk data), and have an FPGA at each endpoint that can react in nanoseconds. Skip any of these and you're systematically slower than the desks that have them, which means you'll only get adverse fills.">Colocate at both venues, lease a microwave + hollow-core path between them, and put an FPGA endpoint on each side that speaks a compact signal protocol.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="A cloud region in Chicago might shave a few µs off your existing system but doesn't help with the Aurora↔Carteret leg, which is the binding constraint.">Move the Python backtests to a cloud region in Chicago for proximity.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Public exchange APIs can't deliver the latency required; you'd be at multi-ms behind firms that have the dedicated infrastructure. The arb would not be viable on public APIs.">Subscribe to both venues' public REST APIs over HTTPS.</li>
