  <!-- ===================== §9 SCENARIOS ===================== -->
  <h2>9. Check your understanding (scenarios)</h2>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S1. A summer ice storm in Pennsylvania bends a microwave tower 2 degrees out of alignment. The link still passes signal but with high error rate. What happens to dependent strategies?</summary>
    <p>
      The link's forward error correction will compensate up to a point — but
      retransmits and dropped messages add latency, and the receiver-side
      strategy will see inconsistent state ("I got the alpha bit for some
      seconds, missed it for others"). Most HFT desks running on a primary
      microwave link have an automatic failover: if error rate or latency
      crosses a threshold, traffic re-routes to a secondary microwave path or
      to hollow-core fiber, accepting the ~1 ms penalty. The strategy
      meanwhile scales itself down or pauses while the failover stabilizes.
      Tower-alignment alerts are a real operational discipline; carriers like
      McKay treat them as priority pages.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S2. A teammate proposes "let's bypass McKay and build our own microwave route from Aurora to Carteret." Walk through the major obstacles before they spend any money.</summary>
    <p>
      Five hard problems, roughly in cost order. (1) <strong>Tower siting</strong>:
      25+ tower sites must be leased or built across multiple states with
      land-use approvals — years of permitting per site. (2) <strong>FCC
      licensing</strong>: each microwave hop requires individual FCC frequency
      authorization; the band is already heavily licensed and incumbents have
      priority. (3) <strong>Capex</strong>: ~$5–10M for the towers and amplifiers
      alone, before integration. (4) <strong>Operations</strong>: 24/7 alignment
      monitoring, weather response, repair crews; carrier-grade ops is a
      headcount problem. (5) <strong>Time-to-market</strong>: 18–36 months
      assuming no FCC delays. Conclusion: unless you're at the very largest
      desk (Jump scale) and have a multi-year horizon, renting from McKay or
      Anova is structurally cheaper, faster, and operationally simpler.
      The build-it-yourself math almost never works.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S3. You sit at a desk with a Premium-tier McKay subscription on CME↔Carteret. A new firm shows up paying for the same Premium tier. What does that mean for your alpha?</summary>
    <p>
      You now share the link's queue with another fast participant who sees
      the same signal at the same time. The microwave link itself isn't
      slower, but the <em>population of equally-fast competitors</em> just
      grew by one. Pre-trade signal value erodes accordingly — what used to
      be your alpha is now a tied race with the new entrant, with the venue's
      matching engine being the tiebreaker. Mitigation paths: (a) move
      faster <em>at the endpoints</em> via FPGA tick-to-trade and queue-priority
      strategies, (b) move to a more exclusive private microwave route if you
      can justify the capex, or (c) shift to strategies whose alpha doesn't
      live on first-fill. The common-carrier model is great for getting onto
      the link cheaply; the downside is that everyone else on it is just as
      well-positioned as you are.
    </p>
  </details>

